### How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | How it works |
|-------|-------------|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class) | Services like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>` are injected via the primary constructor. The class is resolved from the DI container per invocation. |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | DI services can appear directly as method parameters. The SDK resolves them automatically. |

**Key rule:** Injected parameters (`CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<T>`, and any DI-registered service) are **invisible to the LLM** — they don't appear in the tool's JSON schema. Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the model.

In the example above, `HttpClient` and `ILogger<ApiTools>` are constructor-injected because `AddHttpClient()` registers them in DI. The SDK instantiates `ApiTools` with those services, but the LLM only sees `userId`, `title`, `body`, etc.

### Scaffold commands

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n MyApiServer
cd MyApiServer
# Replace the generated files with the code above
dotnet build && dotnet run
```
